aux/intelgpu, aux/inteltest and aux/intelprobe follow Linux's i915
driver (drivers/gpu/drm/i915; MIT).  aux/intelgpu's files are named
after the Linux 6.6 files whose work they do, which UPSTREAM lists
with the functions that correspond: the GT (gt/: the PPGTT, GGTT and
private PAT, the engines, execlists and the logical ring context
image, reset, the null render state, the workarounds, slices and EUs,
RC6 and RPS), the objects and contexts (gem/), the display (display/:
the outputs and their modes, the primary plane, CD clock, DP AUX, I2C
over AUX from drm_dp_helper.c, GMBUS, WRPLL, DDI buffers), forcewake
(intel_uncore.c), interrupts (i915_irq.c), clock gating
(intel_clock_gating.c) and EDID (drm_edid.c).  The register, bit and
command definitions are verbatim excerpts of Linux 6.6's headers
(i915_reg.h, gt/intel_gt_regs.h, ...), each with its header's notice;
the null render state (gt/gen8_renderstate.c), the DDI buffer
translations (display/intel_ddi_buf_trans.c) and the context
workarounds (gt/intel_workarounds.c) are 6.6's code as it is there.

Much of the code was first written after Linux v4.4; the notices of
the v4.4 files it came from follow, then those of the 6.6 files it
follows now (scripts/check/i915-upstream.py -n writes that part).

